WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.080
LLM applications are still software systems, but they introduce design risks that traditional application diagrams often miss.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:18.660
They may process untrusted natural language, retrieve external content, call tools, summarize sensitive data, generate code, or make recommendations that affect business decisions.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.960
Traditional threat modeling still matters.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:29.020
Authentication, authorization, secrets handling, logging, data protection, and API security remain important.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:37.720
The difference is that LLM systems add new questions about instructions, context, model behavior, retrieved content, tool permissions, and downstream trust.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:50.340
A team should ask what instructions are trusted, what content is untrusted, what data the model can access, what tools it can call, and what happens if the model is manipulated, confused, or wrong.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.280
These questions turn AI risk into engineering work instead of vague anxiety.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.140
LLM threat modeling helps teams identify risks before they become production incidents.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:07.560
It creates a shared map of how the feature works, what could go wrong, which controls matter, and where testing or monitoring should focus.
